{
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "req_id": "REQ-009",
  "verdict": "needs_more_hardening",
  "summary": "The candidate is materially stronger than a thin scaffold: it implements real REQ-009 command logic, preserves scope, uses the planned namespace, and has meaningful API-level tests for core success and failure paths. It is still not a serious promotion candidate. The acceptance-critical integrity boundary is unresolved: document persistence, audit persistence, and event publication can diverge under failure, and that behavior is not hardened away. Test credibility remains limited to in-memory fakes, so persistence and audit semantics are modeled rather than proven against the intended repository/schema composition.",
  "source_substance": {
    "status": "substantial_but_not_promotion_grade",
    "notes": [
      "The emitted backend slice is substantive: domain status enum, aggregate and field state models, typed request/response DTOs, explicit error taxonomy, application service, and FastAPI router composition are all present.",
      "REQ scope fidelity is good. The implementation directly addresses POST /documents/{id}/validate, explicit human confirmation, da_validare-only transition, corrected field handling, audit emission, and document.validated publication.",
      "The main source weakness is not thinness; it is integrity. save_validation executes before audit writes and before event publication. If audit or event publication fails, the route returns 503 after some side effects may already be committed.",
      "The code explicitly hardens around save failure mutation by cloning the aggregate before mutation, which is useful, but that does not solve cross-adapter consistency."
    ]
  },
  "test_credibility": {
    "status": "meaningful_but_still_limited",
    "notes": [
      "The tests are real and materially improved. They cover the main happy path, omitted-field behavior, invalid source state, missing explicit confirmation, unknown document, unknown field, duplicate field input, generated correlation id fallback, and repository/audit/event failure cases.",
      "The tests assert concrete audit payload content, state transition payloads, and single event publication on success. This is better than superficial route smoke.",
      "Acceptance credibility is still capped because every proof uses in-memory doubles. There is no repository-backed integration test showing validated_value, corrected_by, corrected_at, validated_by, validated_at, and status=validato persisting against the intended schema.",
      "The current failure-path tests actually expose the unresolved integrity problem rather than closing it: audit failure and event publication failure can occur after prior side effects."
    ]
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "status": "mostly_coherent_with_notable_gaps",
    "notes": [
      "README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC are mostly honest about scope and clearly state what this REQ does not own.",
      "The documented commands and emitted file inventory align with the candidate contents.",
      "The promotion language is somewhat ahead of the evidence. The docs describe promotion-ready seams, but the candidate does not prove promotion-grade durability or consistency under real persistence/eventing composition.",
      "The sample curl payload includes supplier_name, while the only exercised document fixture exposes invoice_number and total_amount. That makes the API example weaker than the tested behavior."
    ]
  },
  "promotion_fit": {
    "status": "localized_and_planned_but_not_ready",
    "notes": [
      "Namespace placement matches PLAN ownership: backend.app.validation.commands.validate, with shared audit and event interfaces only where authorized.",
      "The slice is localized and reviewable, which is favorable for later promotion into canonical src/tests targets.",
      "Promotion risk remains high because the design still depends on downstream canonical composition to supply the real integrity boundary. As emitted, the command can return dependency failure after persisting state or after writing audit rows.",
      "Test wiring is still staging-oriented through PYTHONPATH and direct staged-path imports, so canonical promotion would require cleanup and re-verification."
    ]
  },
  "shared_common_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "The added shared modules are limited to audit and event protocols explicitly allowed by PLAN for REQ-009.",
      "This is justified shared extraction, not unjustified common-module sprawl.",
      "The shared interfaces are intentionally small, but they are also minimal to the point that they do not define stronger delivery or transactional guarantees."
    ]
  },
  "runtime_profile_assessment": {
    "status": "architecturally_aligned_but_not_proven",
    "notes": [
      "Local and target-runtime descriptions preserve one business design through dependency injection rather than splitting into a local-only architecture.",
      "That is the correct promotable direction for this REQ.",
      "However, AWS/runtime alignment is still asserted rather than demonstrated. There are no adapter contract or integration tests proving the same semantics with SQLAlchemy/PostgreSQL persistence and SNS/SQS publication.",
      "The unresolved partial-commit behavior is exactly the kind of issue that becomes more serious, not less serious, in the target runtime."
    ]
  },
  "blockers": [
    "Cross-adapter integrity is still unresolved. The implementation can persist document validation, then fail on audit or event publication, returning 503 after partial side effects have already occurred.",
    "There is no repository-backed integration evidence against the intended schema, so acceptance-critical persistence behavior is not proven beyond in-memory modeling.",
    "The candidate is not yet promotion-safe for canonical targets because the main hardening gap is architectural integrity, not formatting or test count."
  ],
  "remaining_risks": [
    "No executed coverage, JUnit, or security reports are provided here; LTC declares the gates, but the gate outcomes are not evidenced in the artifact set.",
    "The curl example is weaker than the tested contract and may mislead reviewers about valid field behavior.",
    "Staging-specific import/setup assumptions remain in tests and run instructions, increasing friction for promotion into canonical src/tests layout.",
    "Audit and event interfaces do not encode stronger delivery guarantees, so downstream composition choices can easily reintroduce inconsistency if not tightly reviewed."
  ]
}
